				\begin{document}$$\widehat{R}$$\end{document} statistic with a value of 1.1 indicated acceptable convergence. Kaplan–Meier (KM) curves were generated to illustrate survival distributions for both OS and RS-D events. Treatment group comparisons of PROs were conducted using log-rank tests. Forest plots were generated to visualize the treatment effect on OS across different PRO domains. All statistical analyses were conducted using R statistical software (version 4.4.1) with the JMBayes2 (0.5-0), nlme (3.1–165), and survival (3.7-0) packages [36–39]. Conclusions regarding the significance of association were established at a threshold of p < 0.05.

Tables 1 and 2 present the LMM results for change-from-baseline scores and treatment-by-time interaction as well as the OS Cox model, after adjusting for RS-D, change from baseline in corresponding symptoms, and stratification factors. In the LMM derived from the JM, Tisle + PC demonstrated a clinically meaningful and statistically significant improvement in QLQ-C30 GHS/QoL compared to the PC arm (6.29 [95% CI 1.22–11.14]; p = 0.0169). This benefit was accompanied by a significant treatment-by-time interaction (− 2.20 [95% CI − 3.27, − 0.95]; p = 0.0101), indicating that the between-arm difference narrowed over time (Table 1). In the Tisle + nPC arm, a similar trend was observed; however, the numerical magnitude of improvement was slightly larger, with an overall mean change from baseline in GHS/QoL of 10.23 (95% CI 5.47–14.96; p < 0.001), compared to the PC arm (Table 2). The corresponding treatment-by-time interaction was also significant (− 2.53 [95% CI − 3.62, − 1.32]; p < 0.001). In both arms, the corresponding \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
