For LC–specific symptoms assessed via the QLQ-LC13, statistical significance varied across domains. In the Tisle + PC arm, dyspnea-related symptom deterioration was significantly lower than in the PC arm (− 6.92 [95% CI − 11.53, − 2.33]; p = 0.0036), while no significant differences were observed for cough or chest pain (Table 1). Significant treatment-by-time interactions were observed for chest pain (p = 0.0083) and dyspnea (p < 0.001). In the Tisle + nPC arm, chest pain (− 5.63 [95% CI − 9.84, − 1.29]; p = 0.0122) and dyspnea (− 7.57 [95% CI − 12.21, − 3.00]; p = 0.0007) demonstrated significantly less deterioration from baseline compared to the PC arm (Table 2). The treatment-by-time interaction remained significant for both chest pain (1.35 [95% CI 0.04–2.26]; p = 0.0332) and dyspnea (1.52 [95% CI 0.58–2.51]; p < 0.001). The majority of parameters demonstrated strong convergence, with \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
